WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:03.090
(light music)

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:07.240
- We as humans have always
used dogs to help us get

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.640
what we want and what we need.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.640
We make amazing teams because
we've evolved together,

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.270
and they make a massive
difference in conservation

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.890
by finding things that
we couldn't possibly find

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.170
to give us information
to help save species.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.170
Our dogs use their sense
of smell to find everything

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:29.140
from endangered species
to invasive species

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.933
and wildlife crime which is
something we're really proud of.

00:00:34.102 --> 00:00:36.602
(light music)

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:43.263
Come on, guys.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:45.680
Let's go for a walk.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:46.850
I'm Megan Parker.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:48.690
I'm research director and co-founder

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.704
of Working Dogs for Conservation.

00:00:50.704 --> 00:00:51.730
(light music)
Easy.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:52.563
Wait.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.400
OK.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.450
We're an organization that finds dogs

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.280
from rescue situations,
shelters or career change dogs.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.650
We train them as detection
dogs for conservation projects

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.120
around the world.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.950
Right now we're on the way from the kennel

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:12.467
to the armory to pickup
two new elephant tusks

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:16.510
and a leopard skin and
a pangolin to train on.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.460
We're training the dogs on new samples.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.940
That might be finding
scats of endangered species

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:24.420
and doing a population estimate;

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.810
finding live animals that are
endangered that are very rare;

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.920
helping fight wildlife crime in Africa

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.436
by having dogs find ivory
and rhino and bush meat

00:01:33.436 --> 00:01:36.633
and guns and ammunition
that are used for poaching.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.830
- They can detect
microscopic larvae in water.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:50.230
They can detect one species
of fish in a moving stream

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.370
that needs to be removed
from the ecosystem

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.653
before it harms more animals or plants.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.040
You could imagine, like, well,
we could build a computer

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.510
that would sense odors or
sense particles of some object

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:05.620
in the environment except we
can't build anything that fine.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:10.060
Dogs are actually the best
technology and the best able

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.640
to detect something, and
then tell their handler

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:13.473
what they know.

00:02:18.862 --> 00:02:21.362
(light music)

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.620
I started out as a kid
training dogs when I was 10,

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.940
and I just kept that interest.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.620
I became a biologist, and I got my Ph.D.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.400
studying African wild dogs.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.423
This has been a theme, I
guess, for me in my life.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.490
I do have dogs myself.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.770
I actually live with three dogs.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:47.180
Two of them are working
dogs, one is retiring.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.660
He's on the couch, I'm guessing, oh,

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.563
they're both on the couch.
(laughing)

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.640
This morning we started
Tule on an endangered bat

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.940
that she'll be working on.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.450
It's really simple at first.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:05.203
When she smells the bat,
she sits and gets her toy.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:08.190
Let's go to work.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.240
Yes, good girl!

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.480
Go!

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:16.313
Get it, look.

00:03:17.289 --> 00:03:19.870
And then we move to really
complicated scenarios

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:23.070
where the dogs learn to find the scent

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:26.253
of an endangered animal in
a very hard to find place.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.396
Most of our dogs come from shelters

00:03:29.396 --> 00:03:32.650
or from a situation
where somebody can't keep

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:34.837
a dog like this.

00:03:34.837 --> 00:03:38.460
We look at anywhere from
one to two thousand dogs

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.950
before we find a dog
that makes our standards

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.530
and can make it to the field

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.040
as a working dog for conservation.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.020
We're gonna run Utah on the kick fox scat.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.870
Is there anything in
particular you want to work on?

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:52.726
- He's solid, yeah, he knows ...

00:03:52.726 --> 00:03:55.770
- These dogs have really high
energy, they want to play

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.946
with a toy, and without a job,
their energy gets misdirected

00:03:58.946 --> 00:04:02.040
into tearing up your
couch or killing the cat

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.780
or jumping through a
window or chasing whatever.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.360
So these dogs are really
hard to have as pets,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.270
but when they have a job
and have a great home,

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:11.933
it's an awesome combination.

00:04:14.362 --> 00:04:16.960
- Hi, orb, oh my gosh, I know,

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:19.110
we haven't worked
together since canarosis.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.610
- A lot of the dogs that
I've personally trained,

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.010
I thought it would be really hard at first

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:25.320
to have them live on another continent,

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.470
but the care and the bonds
that they have formed

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.220
with their scouts and how hard they work

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.360
and the kind of work that they do

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.097
when I see them there working,

00:04:34.097 --> 00:04:36.453
it's an awesome life for them.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.730
Our work is important, it
pairs these two things I care

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:46.422
a lot about which is taking
dogs that are on death row

00:04:46.422 --> 00:04:49.350
and then they get to amplify
all this conservation

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.000
that we care about that we want to do.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.970
The dogs are making the
world a better place;

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.100
they really are making wildlife safer

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.573
in places where they work.

00:04:59.119 --> 00:05:01.619
(light music)

